g>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\n# Example Input\n-----------\nText:\n\nEntities\n\nid,entity,description\n5,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n1883,RESOLUTION IV DESIGN,\"**Comprehensive Summary of Resolution IV Design**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nResolution IV designs are a specific class of experimental designs utilized primarily in the context of screening experiments. Their main purpose is to enable the estimation of main effects while allowing for the aliasing of two-factor interactions. This characteristic makes them particularly useful when the number of experimental runs is limited, as they provide a balance between the ability to estimate significant effects and the practical constraints of experimental resources.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**\nIn a Resolution IV design, the model can be expressed as:\n\\[ Y = \\mu + \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\beta_i X_i + \\sum_{j < i} \\beta_{ij} X_i X_j + \\epsilon \\]\nwhere \\( Y \\) is the response variable, \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean, \\( \\beta_i \\) are the main effect coefficients, \\( \\beta_{ij} \\) are the interaction effect coefficients, \\( X_i \\) are the factors, and \\( \\epsilon \\) represents the random error. The design structure allows for the estimation of main effects (\\( \\beta_i \\)) while two-factor interactions (\\( \\beta_{ij} \\)) are aliased with each other.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nResolution IV designs are typically structured as fractional factorial designs, where the total number of runs is a fraction of the full factorial design. For example, a \\( 2^{k-p} \\) design, where \\( k \\) is the number of factors and \\( p \\) indicates the fraction of the full design, can be employed. The design is constructed such that the main effects are not confounded with each other or with the two-factor interactions.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of Resolution IV designs relies on several key assumptions:\n1. The effects are additive, meaning that interactions beyond two factors are negligible.\n2. The errors are normally distributed and have constant variance (homoscedasticity).\n3. The observations are independent of one another.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nResolution IV designs are particularly applicable in situations where:\n- The primary goal is to screen a large number of factors to identify the most significant ones.\n- There is a limited number of experimental runs available.\n- The researcher is willing to accept the aliasing of two-factor interactions, as the focus is primarily on main effects.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of data from a Resolution IV design typically involves:\n1. Performing an analysis of variance (ANOVA) to assess the significance of main effects.\n2. Estimating the effects using least squares estimation.\n3. Conducting post-hoc tests or further analysis to explore significant factors.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Efficient use of resources by requiring fewer runs compared to full factorial designs.\n- Ability to identify significant main effects in the presence of limited experimental runs.\n- Flexibility in application across various fields, including industrial and agricultural experiments.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- The main limitation is the aliasing of two-factor interactions, which can obscure the understanding of interaction effects.\n- Not suitable for experiments where interactions are expected to be significant and need to be estimated accurately.\n- Potential for misinterpretation of results if the assumptions are violated.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated designs include Resolution III designs, which allow for the estimation of main effects but confound them with two-factor interactions. Alternatives such as full factorial designs or higher resolution designs (e.g., Resolution V) can be considered when resources permit and when interactions are of interest.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing a Resolution IV design, researchers should carefully consider the selection of factors and the potential for interaction effects. It is crucial to ensure that the design is appropriately randomized to mitigate bias and that the experimental conditions are controlled.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nMany statistical software packages, such as R, Minitab, and JMP, provide tools for designing and analyzing Resolution IV designs. These tools facilitate the construction of the design matrix and the analysis of variance.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nResolution IV designs are commonly used in industrial settings for quality improvement, in agricultural research for optimizing crop yields, and in pharmaceutical studies for screening formulations. They serve as a foundational tool in the design of experiments where the goal is to efficiently identify key factors influencing a response variable.\",6\r\n2103,TWO-FACTOR INTERACTIONS,\"### Two-Factor Interactions\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nTwo-factor interactions refer to the combined effect of two design factors on the response variable in an experiment. They are crucial in understanding how the interplay between factors influences the outcome, particularly in complex experimental designs such as the Spin Coating Experiment. The identification and analysis of these interactions allow researchers to gain insights into the underlying mechanisms affecting the response variable.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation**  \nIn a typical linear model, the effect of two factors \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) on a response variable \\( Y \\) can be expressed as:\n\\[\nY = \\mu + \\alpha_A + \\alpha_B + \\alpha_{AB} + \\epsilon\n\\]\nwhere:\n- \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean,\n- \\( \\alpha_A \\) and \\( \\alpha_B \\) are the main effects of factors \\( A \\) and \\( B \\),\n- \\( \\alpha_{AB} \\) represents the interaction effect between factors \\( A \\) and \\( B \\),\n- \\( \\epsilon \\) is the random error term.\n\n**Design Layout**  \nIn experimental designs, two-factor interactions are typically assessed using factorial designs, where all combinations of the levels of the two factors are tested. For example, in a \\( 2^2 \\) factorial design, each factor has two levels, resulting in four experimental conditions.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe analysis of two-factor interactions assumes:\n1. Independence of observations.\n2. Normality of the residuals.\n3. Homogeneity of variances across treatment combinations (homoscedasticity).\n4. Linearity of the relationship between factors and the response variable.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nTwo-factor interactions should be considered when:\n- The experimental design includes multiple factors that may influence the response.\n- Prior knowledge suggests that the interaction between factors is likely to be significant.\n- The goal is to optimize a response variable based on multiple influencing factors.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nTo analyze two-factor interactions, researchers typically employ Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) techniques. The ANOVA table will include terms for the main effects and the interaction effect, allowing for hypothesis testing regarding their significance.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \n- Provides a more comprehensive understanding of the factors affecting the response variable.\n- Can reveal synergistic or antagonistic effects between factors that would not be apparent when examining main effects alone.\n- Enhances the precision of parameter estimation by accounting for interactions.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \n- Increased complexity in the analysis and interpretation of results.\n- Requires a larger sample size to adequately estimate interaction effects, which can lead to increased costs and resource use.\n- Potential for aliasing, where interactions may be confounded with other effects, complicating the analysis.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nTwo-factor interactions are commonly analyzed within the framework of factorial designs, but can also be explored using response surface methodology (RSM) for optimization purposes. Other alternatives include fractional factorial designs, which allow for the study of interactions with fewer experimental runs.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing two-factor interactions in experiments, researchers should ensure that the design is adequately powered to detect interaction effects. Careful planning of the experimental layout and randomization is essential to minimize bias and confounding.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nStatistical software packages such as R, SAS, and Minitab provide tools for conducting ANOVA and analyzing two-factor interactions. These tools often include graphical outputs, such as interaction plots, to aid in the interpretation of results.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nTwo-factor interactions are widely used in various fields, including materials science (e.g., Spin Coating Experiments), agriculture (e.g., studying the effects of fertilizer type and irrigation level on crop yield), and industrial processes (e.g., examining the impact of temperature and pressure on product quality). Understanding these interactions is essential for optimizing processes and improving outcomes in experimental research.\",2\r\n1882,MONTGOMERY AND RUNGER,\"Montgomery and Runger are authors who discuss the methodology of designing experiments, particularly focusing on resolution IV designs in their 1996 publication.\",1\r\n2188,STANDARD 32-RUN DESIGN,The standard 32-run design is a regular factorial design that provides orthogonality and precision in estimating model coefficients.,1\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n2572,RESOLUTION IV DESIGN,RESOLUTION III DESIGN,\"Resolution IV designs are an advancement over resolution III designs, allowing for more complex experimental setups\",9\r\n2980,TWO-FACTOR INTERACTIONS,RESOLUTION IV DESIGN,Resolution IV designs are specifically designed to estimate main effects while aliasing two-factor interactions,8\r\n2571,MONTGOMERY AND RUNGER,RESOLUTION IV DESIGN,Montgomery and Runger authored a publication discussing the methodology and applications of resolution IV designs in experiments,7\r\n2982,RESOLUTION IV DESIGN,STANDARD 32-RUN DESIGN,The standard 32-run design is a benchmark for comparison against resolution IV designs in terms of precision and aliasing,7\r\n\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT sev